# 11. `commit_validations` Table — Corrected Purpose & Proper Implementation

## Background

> See Reconciliation Notes at the top of this document. The earlier DB Analysis recommendation to remove this table was **incorrect** — its actual purpose is to store manual override decisions on commit compliance/validation status, including who requested and approved the override.

## Database Design

```sql
CREATE TABLE commit_validations (
    id                       BIGINT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,

    -- Identity (composite key)
    team_key                 VARCHAR(50)  NOT NULL,
    fix_version               VARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
    commit_sha                 VARCHAR(40)  NOT NULL,

    -- Override Details
    overridden_status           VARCHAR(50)  NULL,   -- the manually-set status
    override_reason             TEXT NULL,
    override_requested_by       VARCHAR(255) NULL,
    override_requested_at        TIMESTAMP NULL,
    override_approved_by         VARCHAR(255) NULL,
    override_approved_at          TIMESTAMP NULL,

    -- Audit
    created_by                    VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    created_date                   TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    updated_by                      VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    updated_date                     TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,

    CONSTRAINT uq_commit_validations
        UNIQUE (team_key, fix_version, commit_sha)
);

CREATE INDEX idx_commit_validations_lookup
    ON commit_validations(team_key, fix_version, commit_sha);
```

## Functional Requirements

| # | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-11.1 | Retain and properly implement the `commit_validations` table using the schema above (correcting/aligning any existing partial schema to match). |
| FR-11.2 | The composite key is `(team_key, fix_version, commit_sha)` — this is the lookup key everywhere this table is consulted. |
| FR-11.3 | During the "fetch overrides" step of Generate Report / commit analysis, query this table for any existing manual overrides matching the current team + fix version + commit SHAs being processed. |
| FR-11.4 | When a manual override exists for a commit, its `overridden_status` must take precedence over the system-computed compliance status for that commit in the analysis output. |
| FR-11.5 | This table is independent of — and must not be confused with — the `commit_comments` table, which is being removed (its data merged into `commits.commit_message` per the DB Analysis document, unchanged by this bundle). |
| FR-11.6 | Provide a service method (e.g., `getOverridesForCommits(teamKey, fixVersion, List<String> commitShas)`) that batch-fetches applicable overrides in a single query — do not query per-commit in a loop. |

## Acceptance Criteria

```
AC-11.1
GIVEN a manual override exists for commit SHA "abc123..." with overridden_status = "COMPLIANT"
AND the system's computed status for that commit would otherwise be "NON_COMPLIANT"
WHEN commit analysis runs
THEN the final status used is "COMPLIANT" (the override wins)

AC-11.2
GIVEN no override exists for a given commit
WHEN commit analysis runs
THEN the system-computed status is used as-is

AC-11.3
GIVEN 200 commits are being analyzed for a team + fix version
WHEN overrides are fetched
THEN a single batch query retrieves all applicable overrides
— not 200 individual queries

AC-11.4
GIVEN the commit_validations schema is reviewed
WHEN compared against the existing table (if partially implemented)
THEN all fields listed in FR-11.1 are present and correctly typed

AC-11.5
GIVEN the commit_comments table removal proceeds separately (per DB Analysis doc)
WHEN commit_validations is reviewed
THEN it remains untouched and fully functional — the two tables serve different purposes
```

