# 14. `classifyCommit` Method — Condition Order Investigation

## Background

Within `classifyCommit()`, a condition checking roughly "commit does not exist on master AND masterMatch == null" is suspected of being evaluated too early in the method, potentially causing incorrect classification for the common case (where a commit DOES exist on master). This needs investigation and correction now that the method's inputs are changing (Section 13).

## Investigation Task

This is explicitly flagged by the business owner as requiring analysis rather than a predetermined fix. The implementer must:

1. Read the full `classifyCommit()` method and enumerate every conditional branch in order.
2. Identify the specific condition referenced ("not existed on master" combined with "masterMatch == null").
3. Determine, given the updated inputs from Section 13, which classification outcome this condition is meant to represent, and how frequently each branch is expected to be hit in real data (the expectation is that "exists on master" is the common case).
4. Reorder the conditional chain so that the most common, primary classification path is evaluated first, and edge-case/fallback conditions (like the one flagged) are evaluated later — without changing the correctness of any individual condition's logic.
5. Add inline comments explaining the reasoning for the final order, so future readers understand why the conditions are sequenced as they are.

## Functional Requirements

| # | Requirement |
|---|---|
| FR-14.1 | Enumerate and document all conditional branches in `classifyCommit()` before making changes. |
| FR-14.2 | Reorder conditions so common-case logic (commit exists on master) precedes rare edge-case logic (commit not on master AND masterMatch is null), unless doing so would change correctness — correctness takes priority over ordering. |
| FR-14.3 | Do not alter the internal logic of any individual condition without explicit confirmation that it's part of this fix — only reorder unless a genuine bug is found in the condition itself. |
| FR-14.4 | Add code comments explaining the final condition order and reasoning. |
| FR-14.5 | Validate the reordering against a representative sample of components (a mix of clean merges, reverted commits, cherry-picks, and direct commits) to confirm classification outcomes are correct or improved. |

## Acceptance Criteria

```
AC-14.1
GIVEN the classifyCommit() method is reviewed
WHEN all conditional branches are enumerated
THEN a clear list of conditions and their order is documented (in code comments
or a brief note accompanying the PR)

AC-14.2
GIVEN the conditions are reordered
WHEN a commit that exists on master is classified
THEN it is correctly identified via the primary/common-case path
— not incorrectly falling through to the edge-case branch

AC-14.3
GIVEN a commit that genuinely does not exist on master
AND masterMatch is null
WHEN classification runs
THEN it is still correctly handled by the edge-case branch,
now evaluated later in the sequence

AC-14.4
GIVEN the reordering is complete
WHEN a representative sample of components (reverted, cherry-picked,
direct commits, clean merges) is tested
THEN classification results are correct for all sample cases

AC-14.5
GIVEN the final code is reviewed
WHEN the condition order is inspected
THEN inline comments explain why the conditions are sequenced as they are
```

